            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your house is from the mid-70's and was constructed based on the building envelope, air tightness and ventilation requirements from that time. In fact, it still performs relatively well today.



Current building science recognizes the "Build Tight, Ventilate Right" theme, where the house is built super air tight, and a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) unit is integrated to efficiently breathe (ventilate) the house. For your house, it was built relatively air tight and uses mechanical "exhaust only" spot ventilation (range hood, bathroom fan &amp; clothes dryer).



The results of my air test, compared to the internal heated volume of your house would indicate there is room to reduce the air leakage by about 20% before the house would require additional mechanical ventilation. I think about a 10% reduction is about the best you could hope for without taking on a major full house envelope renovation. The areas we identified include:



** Air leakage associated with the wall plugs in the exterior walls of the house



** A hole through into the attic in the spare bedroom closet



** Likely through the crawlspace walls &amp; header space below



** An intentional hole is the 6" chimney and 4" combustion air supply for the boiler



** The current woodstove draws it's combustion air from inside the house, so there will be some air being pulled down through that wood stove. Also consider that each time you use the wood stove, all of the air going up the 6" chimney is being pulled from inside your house - warm air from around the woodstove being sucked up &amp; out, while cold air is being sucked back into the house and drawn across the floor towards the woodstove (likely why you feel cold on your back when sitting down there).



Savings:

If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $ 227 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 9 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.</airSeal>
                <exposedFloors>

This upgrade &amp; the associated savings are included with the foundation upgrade - please see that section of this report.</exposedFloors>
                <foundation>

The foundation of this house has multiple elevations &amp; heights, and I discussed that I will be evaluating it based on an average height (the way our software can understand it). The walls are comprised of a lower concrete wall (pinned to bedrock) that has 2" (R8) of expanded polystyrene foam board insulation on the interior. On top of these concrete walls is a 2x6@16" pony wall that has R20 batt insulation and most of it has an additional 1" (R5) of expanded polystyrene foam board insulation on the interior.



The crawlspace is heated (and the current boiler is located there) and you have started to insulate some of the floor above (primarily for the main floor bathroom). There is a lot of floor area above this foundation &amp; I'm thinking you may have better success upgrading (increasing) the insulation on the walls. I like the idea of continuous spray foam from the header space all the way down to the bedrock (including filling the exposed floor extensions below the closets and the stairwell). 2" of spray foam would represent an adequate vapour barrier, add R10 to the walls (put another R20 in the header) and reduce any air leakage .......... helping to better condition this space so the floor above will feel warmer.



For our software, there are some "picky limitations" on when we evaluate these foundations as a basement, crawlspace or as a slab-on-grade (bedrock). The best fit for your house was for me to model as a heated crawlspace although it has close to enough average height to be deemed a basement. Rather than fuss too much about it - I have modeled the heated foundation as a crawlspace (the best fit for the software) however we will recognize for you the higher rebate levels that we provide for basements.



Savings:

By adding R10 of continuous insulation to 100% of the foundation walls and R20 to the header space, you could potentially save $ 253 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 10 GJ, and qualify for up to an $ 875 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



</foundation>
                <windows />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is heated with a combination oil boiler that also heats your domestic hot water, with a 178 litre external storage buffer tank. The boiler has a 6" flue vent &amp; there is a 4" open combustion air supply into the crawlspace. The online specs for that boiler indicate it had an Energy Star performance rating of 81.0% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE).



I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? 95% AFUE propane combi boiler to heat all of your house and provide high efficiency on-demand domestic hot water. This system will direct vent to the outside and will no longer require the 6" chimney or the 4" combustion air supply pipe.



Savings:

By upgrading to an ENERGY STAR?????? 95% AFUE propane combi boiler to heat all of your house and provide high efficiency on-demand domestic hot water, you could potentially save $ 1,680 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 63 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



OTHER OPTIONS

1. As your current boiler is rated at 81% AFUE and your Princess PE32 has a rated peak efficiency of 80%, I ran a quick calculation to see how much the software would indicate the $$ savings are if you used the wood stove to heat 1,000 ft2 of your home for 50% of the heating season. This is based on the fuel cost rates outlined in the Additional Comments section of this report. The calculated annual savings are $398.



2. You talked about keeping the existing oil boiler, however installing an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater (with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher) in the crawlspace below the laundry. The estimated annual savings are only about $156, as the hot water "piggy backs" on the boiler during most of the heating season, so the real savings come from the late spring, summer &amp; early fall when the oil boiler would be "short cycling" just to provide domestic hot water. This upgrade would qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.  </HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

Add Wall Insulation to exterior of Main &amp; 2nd Floor Walls



You have a large house with a unique design that has multiple corners, exposed floors, vaults and many of the walls are fully inline with the roof above (ie: no overhang). Because of this, you may find it very expensive to complete an exterior retrofit where you add more insulation &amp; new siding. I mentioned that I would model the estimated annual savings if you were to add R10 to the exterior, bringing the total nominal wall insulation up to R30.



Savings:

This wall insulation upgrade is estimated to save $ 601 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 24 GJ - I sense the payback will be very long, especially if you we5re already not planning to invest in a new siding upgrade.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, master bathroom fan &amp; the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 6.8 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent - your current oil boiler and the wood stove are both chimney vented &amp; the wood stove does not have a dedicated/sealed combustion air supply.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:



        - Kitchen range hood fan

        - 1 bathroom fan (the second one on the main floor is vented into the crawlspace)

        - A clothes dryer

 

If the bathroom on the main floor is used for bathing/showering, that exhaust fan should be redirected to the outside of the house.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $0.91 per litre 

Propane - $0.61

Wood - $420.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



